oval.\n\nBut careful: \"prioritize failures before successes\" means that when choosing which to keep, we prefer failures. So successes are more likely to be evicted. Then among failures, we keep the most recent ones. Among successes, also keep most recent. Then tie-breaking by signature.\n\nImplementation: after processing all trials and building list of all entries (with dedup for failures), we have a list possibly exceeding capacity. Then we need to select up to capacity entries. We can sort all entries by a composite key: first key: is_failure? (True=higher priority, so maybe sort by (is_failure=False, index descending?) Actually we want to keep failures first, then recency descending. So we can sort all entries by: (-is_failure, -last_trial, signature) but careful: is_failure is boolean, we want failures before successes, so we can sort by (not success, -last_trial, signature). Then take the first capacity entries. Then we need to restore chronological order: sort those selected entries by last_trial ascending.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to produce output: memory array of objects with last_trial, reflection, signature, success. Also signatures list (just the signatures in the same order as memory).\n\nWe must also validate input? Raise ValueError if structurally invalid? Typically we trust the input is valid per schema, but maybe we should check types.\n\nWe need to remember that case is the payload only. So we don't have case_id, etc.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- def solve(case):\n    # case is dict with \"capacity\" and \"trials\"\n    capacity = case[\"capacity\"]\n    trials = case[\"trials\"]\n\n    # process trials in order\n    memory_by_signature = {}  # for failures only: signature -> entry (dict with last_trial, reflection, signature, success)\n    all_entries = []  # list of all entries (for successes, and failures after dedup)\n    # We'll maintain a list of all entries (including failures that may be updated).\n    # We'll track failure indices.\n\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        sig = trial[\"signature\"]\n        ref = trial[\"reflection\"]\n        suc = trial[\"success\"]\n        if not suc:  # failure\n            # check if signature already in failures dict\n            if sig in memory_by_signature:\n                # update existing entry\n                index_in_all = memory_by_signature[sig]  # the index in all_entries list\n                entry = all_entries[index_in_all]\n                entry[\"reflection\"] = ref\n                entry[\"last_trial\"] = i\n                # success remains false\n            else:\n                entry = {\"last_trial\": i, \"reflection\": ref, \"signature\": sig, \"success\": False}\n                all_entries.append(entry)\n                memory_by_signature[sig] = len(all_entries) - 1\n        else:  # success\n            entry = {\"last_trial\": i, \"reflection\": ref, \"signature\": sig, \"success\": True}\n            all_entries.append(entry)\n            # no dedup for successes\n\n    # Now all_entries may exceed capacity\n    if len(all_entries) > capacity:\n        # sort to select which to keep\n        # sorting key: (-success, -last_trial, signature) -> but success boolean, we want failures (success=False) first\n        # So key = (success, -last_trial, signature) and then sort ascending, because success=False (0) < success=True (1)\n        # Actually failures have success=False=0, successes success=True=1. So sorting by (success, -last_trial, signature) ascending will put failures first.\n        sorted_entries = sorted(all_entries, key=lambda e: (e[\"success\"], -e[\"last_trial\"], e[\"signature\"]))\n        selected = sorted_entries[:capacity]\n        # Restore chronological order\n        selected.sort(key=lambda e: e[\"last_trial\"])\n    else:\n        selected = all_entries\n\n    # Build signatures list\n    signatures = [e[\"signature\"] for e in selected]\n\n    return {\"memory\": selected, \"signatures\": signatures}\n\nBut we need to ensure we don't mutate case. We are creating new dicts for entries, so fine.\n\nEdge cases: If capacity is 0? Then return empty memory and signatures.\n\nWhat about dedup for failures? We replaced the entry's reflection but kept the same position in all_entries? But the order of all_entries is the order of first occurrence. That may affect chronological order later? Actually the final chronological order is by last_trial, which we set to the new index. So after selection, we sort by last_trial. That's fine. However, note that if a failure is replaced, its position in all_entries remains the first index, but its last_trial is updated to later index. That is fine because the final sort uses last_trial. The initial all_entries list order doesn't matter for final chronological order because we sort.\n\nOne nuance: When we replace a failure entry, we are not inserting a new entry; we update the existing one. This means the total count of entries might stay the same? Actually if we have a new failure with same signature, we don't add a new entry, we update. So number of entries might be less than number of trials. That seems consistent with dedup.\n\nBut consider: If a failure appears, then later a success with same signature appears, we have two entries: one failure (updated) and one success. That's fine.\n\nNow, about \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" – does it mean we should only keep one failure per signature in the entire memory? Yes, we are doing that by updating in place.\n\nNow, about capacity binding: \"prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" Our sorting key does that: first by success (False before True), then by recency descending (-last_trial), then signature. That ensures failures are kept over successes; among failures, the most recent first; among successes, most recent first; ties by signature.\n\nNow, after selection, we restore chronological order: sort by last_trial ascending. That is correct.\n\nWe should also handle the case where there are duplicate successes? The spec doesn't mention dedup for successes, so we keep them all. But then capacity may cut them.\n\nNow test with given fixtures? Let's simulate with first fixture to see if logic matches expected. We don't have expected output, but we can reason.\n\nFixture 1: capacity=3, trials:\n\n0: e1, r0, true\n1: e3, r1, false\n2: e0, r2, true\n3: e1, r3, false\n4: e3, r4, false\n5: e1, r5, true\n6: e0, r6, false\n\nProcess:\ni=0: success e1 -> add entry0: (0, r0, e1, true)\ni=1: failure e3 -> add entry1: (1, r1, e3, false)\ni=2: success e0 -> add entry2: (2, r2, e0, true)\ni=3: failure e1 -> signature e1 is in failures? No, e1 failures not yet (only success). So add entry3: (3, r3, e1, false)\ni=4: failure e3 -> e3 already in failures (entry1). Update entry1: last_trial=4, reflection=r4, success false.\ni=5: success e1 -> add entry4: (5, r5, e1, true)\ni=6: failure e0 -> e0 not in failures (only success), add entry5: (6, r6, e0, false)\n\nNow all_entries: \n0: (0, r0, e1, true)\n1: (4, r4, e3, false)  # updated\n2: (2, r2, e0, true)\n3: (3, r3, e1, false)\n4: (5, r5, e1, true)\n5: (6, r6, e0, false)\n\nNow len=6 > cap=3. Sort by (success, -last_trial, signature):\nsuccess values: T=1, F=0. So failures come first.\nFailures: entries 1 (last_trial=4), 3 (3), 5 (6). Sort by -last_trial: 6,4,3 -> signatures: e0 (entry5), e3 (entry1), e1 (entry3). Then successes: entries 0 (0),2(2),4(5). Sort: -last_trial: 5,2,0 -> signatures: e1 (entry4), e0 (entry2), e1 (entry0). Ties within same success? No ties.\nTake top 3: failures: entry5 (e0, last_trial=6), entry1 (e3, last_trial=4), entry3 (e1, last_trial=3). So selected = [e0 failure, e3 failure, e1 failure].\nRestore chronological: sort by last_trial: 3,4,6 -> order: e1 (last=3), e3 (4), e0 (6). Signatures: [e1, e3, e0]. That seems plausible.\n\nNow second fixture: capacity=3, trials:\n0: e2 false\n1: e3 true\n2: e3 false\n3: e1 false\n4: e2 false\n5: e2 false\n6: e3 false\n\nProcess:\ni=0: failure e2 -> add entry0: (0, r0, e2, false)\ni=1: success e3 -> add entry1: (1, r1, e3, true)\ni=2: failure e3 -> e3 not in failures yet (only success), add entry2: (2, r2, e3, false)\ni=3: failure e1 -> add entry3: (3, r3, e1, false)\ni=4: failure e2 -> e2 in failures (entry0), update entry0: last_trial=4, reflection=r4, false\ni=5: failure e2 -> again e2, update entry0: last_trial=5, reflection=r5, false\ni=6: failure e3 -> e3 in failures (entry2), update entry2: last_trial=6, reflection=r6, false\n\nNow all_entries:\n0: (5, r5, e2, false)\n1: (1, r1, e3, true)\n2: (6, r6, e3, false)\n3: (3, r3, e1, false)\n\nlen=4 > cap=3. Sort: failures: entries 0 (5), 2 (6), 3 (3). By -last_trial: 6,5,3 -> signatures: e3, e2, e1. Successes: entry1 (1). Top 3: failures e3 (6), e2 (5), e1 (3). So selected all failures. Restore chronological: 3,5,6 -> e1, e2, e3. Signatures: [e1, e2, e3]. Good.\n\nThird fixture: capacity=3, trials:\n0: e1 false\n1: e3 false\n2: e3 false\n3: e3 false\n4: e0 false\n5: e3 true\n6: e3 false\n\nProcess:\ni=0: failure e1 -> add entry0: (0, r0, e1, false)\ni=1: failure e3 -> add entry1: (1, r1, e3, false)\ni=2: failure e3 -> update entry1: (2, r2, e3, false)\ni=3: failure e3 -> update entry1: (3, r3, e3, false)\ni=4: failure e0 -> add entry2: (4, r4, e0, false)\ni=5: success e3 -> add entry3: (5, r5, e3, true)\ni=6: failure e3 -> update entry1: (6, r6, e3, false)\n\nAll entries:\n0: (0, r0, e1, false)\n1: (6, r6, e3, false)\n2: (4, r4, e0, false)\n3: (5, r5, e3, true)\nlen=4 > cap=3. Sort: failures: entries 0(0),1(6),2(4). -last_trial: 6,4,0 -> e3, e0, e1. Success: entry3(5). Top 3: failures e3, e0, e1. Restore chronological: 0,4,6 -> e1, e0, e3. Signatures: [e1, e0, e3]. That seems plausible.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON output with implementation key and Python source as string.\n\nWe need to escape accordingly. Use triple quotes but careful with JSON string escaping.\n\nWe'll write the code inside a multiline string, then embed as JSON value.\n\nWe must ensure no extraneous text.\n\nLet's generate JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    memory_by_signature = {}\\n    all_entries = []\\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        sig = trial['signature']\\n        ref = trial['reflection']\\n        suc = trial['success']\\n        if not suc:\\n            if sig in memory_by_signature:\\n                idx = memory_by_signature[sig]\\n                entry = all_entries[idx]\\n                entry['reflection'] = ref\\n                entry['last_trial'] = i\\n            else:\\n                entry = {'last_trial': i, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': False}\\n                all_entries.append(entry)\\n                memory_by_signature[sig] = len(all_entries) - 1\\n        else:\\n            entry = {'last_trial': i, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': True}\\n            all_entries.append(entry)\\n    if len(all_entries) > capacity:\\n        sorted_entries = sorted(all_entries, key=lambda e: (e['success'], -e['last_trial'], e['signature']))\\n        selected = sorted_entries[:capacity]\\n        selected.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\\n    else:\\n        selected = all_entries\\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in selected]\\n    return {'memory': selected, 'signatures': signatures}\\n\"}\n\nBut need to ensure double quotes inside Python string are escaped when embedding in JSON. In the Python code, we use single quotes for dict keys, which is fine. The JSON string should contain the Python source with newlines as \\n. Also we must escape backslashes. The code above uses no backslashes. So it's fine.\n\nNow validate that the code only uses standard library. Yes.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer as JSON object with \"implementation\" key.\n\nMake sure not to add any extra spaces before first brace.\n\nLet's output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1722,"completion_tokens":5016,"total_tokens":6738,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4661},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":58},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}